From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MIRAGE <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 16:04:03 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] MIRAGE: gate contract v2: respond, plant_token, and what the
 decoy can do

The policy may now answer a command with an action the decoy takes only if
it said it could. Each request lists those in `supports` and says in `tier`
what the decoy has for the input.

respond. The command runs as an ordinary command on the cmdstack whose
output comes from the responder service (cowrie/mirage_respond.py). It sends
who and where the session is, what the session has changed since it last
asked (the journal), and what is at the paths the command names; it reads
the reply as it arrives. Lines are shown as they come, through the same
pipes and redirections as any command's. A held answer's operations are
checked against this session's own tree and applied before a line of it is
shown. Typed-ahead input queues behind it and Ctrl-C ends it, closing the
connection, which is how the responder learns. Whatever goes wrong before
anything has been shown, the command falls back to what the emulator would
have run, and the session sees nothing of the attempt.

plant_token. The decision's operations (a file holding a token) are applied,
all or none, and the command then runs as usual. What was planted is kept
from every later answer: not shown to the responder, not to be touched by
its operations.

supports. respond is listed only while the responder passed its last
background health check and the session's journal is whole, so the policy
learns availability without calling anything. An action the request did not
list is not taken.

tier gains `absent`: no such program. That is not 'nothing to answer with':
the shell's 'command not found' is the true answer, and only `none` (a
program that is there, which nothing here answers for) is the responder's.
---
